Each of these products moves work or data out of the request that started it.
They differ in the unit they track.
Choose by that unit, then check the delivery promise.

```text
request ──┬─▶ Queue ──────▶ consumer: one job, ack or retry
          ├─▶ Pipeline ───▶ SQL ──▶ R2 table or files
          ├─▶ Workflow ───▶ step ─▶ step ─▶ sleep ─▶ step
          └─▶ K2 stream ──▶ subscription A (reads every record)
                       └──▶ subscription B (reads every record)
```

## Queues track one job at a time

A queue holds messages for a consumer Worker. Cloudflare lists three uses: offload work from a request, send data from Worker to Worker, and buffer or batch writes. [Storage options](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/storage-options/)

- **Delivery:** at least once by default. Give each message a unique id and use it as an [idempotency](/lore/http-responses-need-validation) key. [Delivery guarantees](https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/reference/delivery-guarantees/)
- **Per message:** a consumer can `ack()` or `retry()` each message in a batch. [Batching and retries](https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/configuration/batching-retries/)
- **After retries:** a message moves to a dead letter queue after `max_retries`, three by default. Without a dead letter queue, Cloudflare deletes it. [Dead letter queues](https://developers.cloudflare.com/queues/configuration/dead-letter-queues/)

Cloudflare's rule: "Use Queues when each message is a unit of work that needs to be completed, retried, and tracked individually." [Storage options](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/storage-options/)

In [Alchemy](/skills/learn-alchemy), `Cloudflare.Queues.Queue` declares the queue and `Cloudflare.Queues.Consumer` attaches a Worker, with `deadLetterQueue` and `settings.maxRetries`. A Worker sends through `Cloudflare.Queues.WriteQueue`. [Queue.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/v2.0.0-beta.85/packages/cloudflare/src/Queues/Queue.ts) [Consumer.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/v2.0.0-beta.85/packages/cloudflare/src/Queues/Consumer.ts)

See [Dead letters need a return path](/lore/dead-letters-need-a-return-path) for what the dead letter queue still needs: an owner and a redrive.

## Pipelines load rows into R2

Basin Pipelines is Cloudflare's streaming ingestion product, and it has three parts. Alchemy names it `Cloudflare.Pipelines`. [Basin Pipelines](https://developers.cloudflare.com/basin-pipelines/)

1. A **stream** receives events over HTTP or a Worker [binding](/lore/bindings).
2. A **pipeline** runs SQL over the stream.
3. A **sink** writes Iceberg tables to Basin Catalog, or Parquet and JSON files to R2.

Sinks deliver exactly once. [Sinks](https://developers.cloudflare.com/basin-pipelines/sinks/) Cloudflare lists clickstream analytics, [telemetry](/lore/trace-meaningful-operations) and logs as uses. [Storage options](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/storage-options/)

The output is a table or a set of files in R2 that you query later.

### How rat-stack uses it

Pipelines is the only one of the four that rat-stack declares. `@rat-stack/basin` builds the stream for both [request analytics](/systems/analytics) and join intake. The stream accepts no HTTP input, so only the Worker binding writes to it.

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/basin/src/index.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=14-25 {20-22} title="A private stream beside its bucket"

```

`icebergSink` then copies every row unchanged into one table:

```ts repo=rat-stack path=packages/basin/src/index.ts at=a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725 lines=71-73 title="SELECT * keeps the raw row"

```

[Capture first](/lore/capture-first) explains why the copy stays raw.

## Workflows run steps that resume

A Workflow chains steps, retries failed steps, and persists state for minutes, hours or weeks. It can sleep, and it can wait for an external event such as an approval. [Workflows](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/)

Each step retries on its own, so make each step idempotent. [Rules of Workflows](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/build/rules-of-workflows/) A step that charges a card must first check whether the charge already happened.

In Alchemy, a Workflow body is an [Effect](/lore/effect-basics). `Cloudflare.Workflows.task` runs one named durable step. Its result is persisted and replayed on retries. `sleep`, `sleepUntil` and `waitForEvent` pause the run. The same source describes Workflows as at-least-once. [Workflow.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/v2.0.0-beta.85/packages/cloudflare/src/Workflows/Workflow.ts)

Choose a Workflow when the unit is a process with ordered steps and waits. Each instance has a unique ID and runs many steps. [Rules of Workflows](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workflows/build/rules-of-workflows/)

## K2 keeps a log that many readers replay

K2 is a durable log. Producers append records. Consumers read through subscriptions, and each subscription receives every record. [K2 concepts](https://developers.cloudflare.com/k2/concepts/)

- **Reading does not delete:** records stay until retention ends. Retention goes up to 30 days, and longer by request. [K2](https://developers.cloudflare.com/k2/)
- **Replay:** a new subscription can start at `earliest` and read records produced before it existed. [Delivery guarantees](https://developers.cloudflare.com/k2/reference/delivery-guarantees/)
- **Leases:** a consumer holds a batch for five minutes, then acks, nacks, or loses it to redelivery. [K2 concepts](https://developers.cloudflare.com/k2/concepts/)
- **Limits of the promise:** delivery is at least once per subscription. K2 acks whole batches, does not deduplicate, and does not order records across workers. [Delivery guarantees](https://developers.cloudflare.com/k2/reference/delivery-guarantees/)

Cloudflare says to use K2 when what matters is "that every record is processed rather than the state of any one record." [Storage options](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/storage-options/)

K2 is in public beta on the Workers Paid plan. [K2](https://developers.cloudflare.com/k2/) Alchemy declares it as `Cloudflare.K2.Stream` and `Cloudflare.K2.Subscription`. A stream can take an Effect [Schema](/lore/schemas-define-the-boundary), so consumers receive decoded values. [K2/Stream.ts](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/blob/v2.0.0-beta.85/packages/cloudflare/src/K2/Stream.ts)

## Choose by the unit you track

- **One job that must finish:** Queues. Track and retry each message.
- **Rows you query later:** Pipelines. Load them into R2 as a table.
- **A process with steps and waits:** Workflows. Resume from the last finished step.
- **Events for several independent readers:** K2. Replay them from the log.

Three of the four deliver at least once. Make every consumer and step safe to repeat. [Retry needs idempotent effects](/lore/retry-needs-idempotency) shows how.

## Where rat-stack does without them

Join intake must deliver each contact after the response. It uses no queue. It saves the contact with a pending-delivery marker, and a [cron every five minutes](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/a3f5a14861ce89913e82bea0f7ba02c83e8d4725/apps/site/src/interest/intake-sweep.ts#L13) redrives every marked contact. [Durability](/systems/durability) shows the model tests that check it.

The marker is the recovery record. Cloudflare lists this job, offloading work from a request, as a Queues use. [Storage options](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/storage-options/) [Request lifetime is not durability](/lore/request-lifetime-is-not-durability) explains why a record is needed at all.

